title: Why undo does not step HEAD back
tags: [[Event log]]
kind: rationale
summary: Rolling HEAD back one write would unrecord what the watcher saw — facts still true on disk — so undo reverts in place.

The obvious undo — move HEAD back one write — is wrong as soon as the watcher runs.
Between the change you want back and the present sit the watcher's writes: a file
touched, a directory renamed, a file gone. Rewinding HEAD over them ''unrecords facts
that are still true on disk'', and the metadata falls out of step with the filesystem the
daemon exists to track. The watcher's writes are not yours to undo.

So undo reverts: it writes the inverse of your change onto the current state, and the
watcher's work stays. It first chose between a rollback (when your change was still the
last thing in the log) and a revert — a choice that depended on whether the watcher had
written yet, which nobody controls, and that cost redo its footing when the watcher wrote
between an undo and its redo. Undo and redo are reverts only since October 2026, and the
log is no longer a tree ([[Why the log stops being a tree]]).

The origin a write carries is what makes the distinction possible: the operation
types cannot say it, since a file arriving is a creation like any other
([[Operations and their origin]]).
